  "style": "",
  "text": "The present study was conducted with 933 individuals aged 60 and older, to determine the prevalence of cognitive impairment or risk of dementia in Nepal. It compared the prevalence of cognitive impairment across different age groups, across male and female gender, and across three geographic regions of Nepal: Humla (mountainous region); Kavrepalachowk (hilly region) and Siraha (low-lying region)."
}

{
  "paraId": "4EBE341D",
  "style": "",
  "text": "The prevalence of cognitive impairment or risk of dementia increases monotonically with age in the present study, reflecting a significant age effect, consistent with literature reporting an increasing risk of dementia with age 34, 37. Community-level screening may therefore be the best strategy to recognise the risk of dementia as early as possible, and early interventions could help reduce that risk. New technologies to identify biomarkers may also aid early diagnosis, prevention, and treatment of cognitive decline 38, 39. The global action plan for a public health response to dementia 2017–2025 describes goals and actions to help nations handle the expanding dementia crisis 40; it holds that universal access to care and support for people with dementia and their carers depends on prompt, accurate diagnosis, ideally at the primary care level. Literature suggests that being cognitively, physically, and socially active from midlife reduces the risk of dementia 35. Awareness programmes should therefore focus on risk factors, early signs and symptoms, and healthy lifestyles 35, 40, and on understanding cardiovascular risk factors that increase the risk of dementia 8, 38, thereby promoting healthy lifestyles and risk reduction behaviour in all."
}

{
  "paraId": "594BDE29",
  "style": "",
  "text": "A noticeable geographic effect was observed in the present study, with higher prevalence of cognitive impairment associated with living at higher altitudes. This could be associated with rurality, as people living in mountainous area may have many challenges in their daily life due to the local terrain and may also face difficulties in accessing care facilities. The aetiology of dementia may have significant socio-environmental contributions, as suggested by regional variations in dementia prevalence 41. A systematic literature review comprising 51 studies across the world shown that people living in rural areas had higher prevalence of dementia than those in urban areas 41. Similar findings were noted among Thai and Chinese people living in rural areas, who had higher prevalence of dementia symptoms 22, 42. The literature suggests that LMICs are not well prepare to care for and support the anticipated future rise in the number of dementia patients and need for advance care planning 43. Hence, there is a need to prioritise research in rural and underserved areas, as well as low resource settings areas to further establish the relationship between geographic region and dementia, along with the possible explanation for the differences."
}

